---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Translation Canon"
  description="What we keep consistent across the patch. The names, titles, places, and conventions we lock in early so every line stays in agreement."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'Translation Canon' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Every translation has to make choices the original didn't. Is Vlad
    "Mr. Vlad" or just "Vlad"? Is the currency "Mira" or "Ricchi"? Is
    まじない "incantation" or "spell"? Once we've picked, we have to
    pick the same way every time, or NPCs start contradicting each
    other.
  </p>

  <p>
    Most of these choices are anchored by <strong>Magician's Quest 1</strong>
    (also called Mysterious Times in North America, Enchanted Folk in
    Europe), the same studio's earlier game that did get an English
    release. Whenever Tongari mentions a character, item, or concept
    that exists in Game 1, we use Game 1's official translation.
  </p>

  <div class="fg-cards">
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/translation-canon/npc-names/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>NPC names</h3>
      <p>Who's who, and why some characters' English names look nothing like the Japanese — they're Konami's choices, not ours.</p>
    </a>
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/translation-canon/places-and-currency/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>Places, titles, and currency</h3>
      <p>The town's shops, the royal court, and the currency unit (Mira, not Ricchi).</p>
    </a>
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/translation-canon/glossary/`} class="fg-card">
      <h3>Glossary terms</h3>
      <p>The recurring concepts — magic, incantation, the magic-language keyboard, the auditorium, the dorm, the woods.</p>
    </a>
  </div>

  <h2>How the canon is enforced</h2>
  <p>
    The database tracks every translation. When we apply a glossary
    change — say, deciding まじない is "incantation" rather than "spell"
    — a script scans every dialog entry for the affected JP token and
    canonicalizes the English form. We did this once across the whole
    patch in the
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/message-system/shared-payloads/`}>shared-payload
    canonicalization</a> pass, and we run it again whenever the canon
    shifts.
  </p>

  <p>
    Consistency checks then sweep the database for dialog where a
    glyph or canon term is highlighted with a color tag but the
    surrounding English doesn't match the canonical form. About 700
    entries needed fixing in the initial sweep; ongoing sweeps catch
    drift as we keep editing.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
